## Local SEO for Architecture Practices

Local SEO for an architecture practice should reflect a real office and a credible working area. One office can serve several towns but that doesn't justify cloning the same page with each place name changed.

A Reading practice might have a primary office page for Reading and supporting coverage for Berkshire towns where it can show relevant knowledge or projects. Each useful location page needs distinct planning context, service demand, travel or meeting arrangements and links to appropriate work. [Local SEO](/services/local-seo/) connects those pages with Google Business Profile rather than pretending the practice has offices it doesn't have.

I am based in Reading and work across Berkshire, so the example is practical rather than invented. The same structure applies UK-wide through remote consulting. A practice should be precise about where it is based, where it works and how clients engage.

## Architecture SEO Questions

**Do project portfolio pages help SEO?**

Project portfolio pages help only when they include useful text, project type, location and process. Image galleries alone give search engines too little context and usually rank for nothing beyond a project name.

**Should an architect target planning-permission keywords?**

Yes. Informational planning content is often the highest-volume entry point for prospective clients and can feed enquiries when it connects clearly to the relevant architectural service.

**How long before an architecture site sees results?**

Architecture sites typically need 4 to 6 months to improve for local service terms. Competitive sector terms take longer because the practice needs stronger project evidence, content coverage and authority.

